# Presentation layer

In this repository, controllers live in `webapp` and use classic Spring MVC with
`ModelAndView`.

## Responsibilities

- Receive the HTTP request.
- Extract parameters with `@RequestParam` and `@PathVariable`.
- Delegate logic to the appropriate service.
- Prepare the model for the JSP.
- Handle form binding and validation.

## Dependency injection

Inject service contracts from `service-contracts`, never concrete implementations.
The preferred style in this repository is constructor injection.

```java
@Controller
public class HelloWorldController {

    private final ClassUserService classUserService;

    @Autowired
    public HelloWorldController(final ClassUserService classUserService) {
        this.classUserService = classUserService;
    }
}
```

## Route and view conventions

- HTTP routes are declared in the controller with `@RequestMapping`.
- `WebConfig`'s `ViewResolver` adds `"/WEB-INF/views/"` and `".jsp"`.
- Because of that, controllers should return logical view names whenever possible,
  for example `"index"` or `"helloworld/index"`.

Do not document `WEB-INF/jsp` or assume every handler must return the full JSP
path. In this project, the correct convention is `WEB-INF/views`.

## Real examples from the repository

```java
@RequestMapping(value = "/", method = RequestMethod.GET)
public ModelAndView landing() {
    return new ModelAndView("index");
}

@RequestMapping(value = "/marketplace/{item_id}", method = RequestMethod.GET)
public ModelAndView marketplaceItem(@PathVariable("item_id") final String itemId) {
    final ModelAndView mav = new ModelAndView("marketplace-item");
    mav.addObject("itemId", itemId);
    return mav;
}

@RequestMapping(value = "/class", method = RequestMethod.POST)
public ModelAndView createClassUser(
        @RequestParam("email") final String email,
        @RequestParam("password") final String password,
        @RequestParam("username") final String username) {
    final ClassUser classUser = classUserService.createClassUser(email, password, username);
    final ModelAndView mav = new ModelAndView("helloworld/index");
    mav.addObject("message", "Hello World " + classUser.getUsername());
    return mav;
}
```

## MVC conventions

- `@ModelAttribute` form binding;
- `@Valid` plus `BindingResult`;
- helper `@ModelAttribute` methods that add common model data;
- Spring form taglib in JSPs;
- redirects after successful POSTs when the flow benefits from PRG.

Example of an accepted pattern:

```java
@RequestMapping(value = "/", method = RequestMethod.GET)
public ModelAndView index(@ModelAttribute("registerForm") final UserForm form) {
    return new ModelAndView("index");
}

@RequestMapping(value = "/create", method = RequestMethod.POST)
public ModelAndView create(
        @Valid @ModelAttribute("registerForm") final UserForm form,
        final BindingResult errors) {
    if (errors.hasErrors()) {
        return index(form);
    }
    // map form to service call
    return new ModelAndView("redirect:/...");
}
```

## Do not invent unrelated conventions

- Do not assume an `interfaces/` module; use `service-contracts`.
- Do not place SQL or business rules in the controller.
- Do not use persistence beans directly from `webapp`.

## Rules

- Prefer constructor injection.
- Use `ModelAndView`.
- Inject interfaces from `service-contracts`.
- Let `WebConfig` resolve the view prefix and suffix.
- Keep route names explicit and consistent with the existing feature.
- Use web-layer form objects and validation.
